{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed. I will review the target-state conversation model at exact head bb170cf2f0bb8a2d2ecc945824231b6d57faca7a, naming artifact 9f57f3172abbb1a3a73cffadd9a2a3c7795cf043 at notes/2026-08-11-conversation-model.md.\n\nI will judge the model's fidelity to the canonical conversation-for-action and rework loop, and record the architecture, security and simplification conclusions, then report approved or changes-requested for this exact head.\n\nThree things I will check specifically, because they are where a model of this loop goes wrong in ways the prose hides:\n\nThe trace mechanism. If the model requires a changes-requested verdict to be traced to its implementation promise, it must specify the exact structural path and require it to use only fold-effective records. There are at least two plausible paths — through the artifact the review names, or through the review request's Rests-On chain — and they disagree precisely in the messy cases: re-anchored artifacts and refiled review requests. Without that pinned, the conditions have nothing to be written against.\n\nDissolution, not just the happy loop. Entry into rework is the easy half. I will look for what ends or interrupts it: a review request superseded and refiled mid-review, which cancels the reviewer's commitment and leaves a verdict effective but bound to nothing; a second consecutive changes-requested cycle, which must not accumulate state; and a changes-requested arriving after satisfaction was already ratified or after the promise was superseded, which must be a no-op. These are exactly where \"waiting on performer\" rows become phantoms.\n\nVerdict detection. I measured this log today: refused reports carry their verdict in body.verdict, in body.status, in text only, and in mixed case. Any transition keyed on one field and an exact lowercase match will miss real records, and any transition reading projection.statements rather than projection.reviews will admit reports the fold refused.\n\nI will also check that \"live\" is used to mean not-retired per the fold's own signal rather than not-stale, since a reworked promise's earlier report is normally stale but unretired, and conflating the two is a known trap in this log."}